#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# asymptotic_product

#% text
# `asymptotic_product(f, [k, a, b], [x, x0, m])` gives the expansion of the closed-form product of f over k to order m in x at x0; `x -> x0` gives the leading term.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `asymptotic_product(f, k, [k, inf, m])` expands the indefinite product P with P(k + 1)/P(k) = f(k) and P -> 1, from the expansion of the indefinite sum of log(f) (`asymptotic_sum`).

#% item
# As in `series` at `inf`, order m keeps every term whose power of 1/x is at most m, fractional powers included: `(k^(13/4) - 1)/(k^(13/4) + 1)` gives `1 + 8/(9 k^(9/4))` at order 3 and adds `k^(-13/4)` at order 4.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
asymptotic_product(1 + a/k^2, [k, 1, 3], [a, 0, 2])
#% output
#= 1 + (49/36)*a + (7/18)*a^2

#% cell
asymptotic_product(k + m, [k, 1, 4], [m, 0, 1])
#% output
#= 24 + 50*m

#% cell
asymptotic_product((k + 1)/k, [k, 1, n], [n, 2, 1])
#% output
#= 1 + n

#% cell
asymptotic_product((k^(13/4) - 1)/(k^(13/4) + 1), k, [k, inf, 4])
#% output
#= 1 + 8/(9*k^(9/4)) + k^(-13/4)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `product`, `series`, `asymptotic_sum`
